  "analysis": "Market read: the tape is corrective. Over 20 sessions most assets are down (A10 -5.0%, A12 -8.0%, A11 -3.1%, A06 -3.1%, A15 -4.8%, A05 -4.7%), with energy (A03 +8.5%, A13 +12.6%) and health (A04 +3.9%, A07 +6.5%) the only bright spots; 1-day moves are mixed. Forecast medians are uniformly tiny and have no directional skill, so I do not chase them — the reliable signal is interval width. The portfolio sits in a shallow -2.4% drawdown with no forced de-risking, but the broad recent weakness supports keeping the existing defensive posture (cash 20.6%, bonds 14.5%) rather than re-risking. Main risks: (1) concentration in the lowest-vol assets (A02, A06, A09) means we lag if the tape bounces; (2) the zero weights in the widest-interval names (A12, A01, A07, A14) forgo upside, but those names carry 8-12% 5-day widths and 18-29% vol with no median edge, so the baseline's small weights are not worth the idiosyncratic risk; (3) gold at 2% vs 5.3% baseline sacrifices some diversification, justified by its 21% vol and 7.0% width. Changes: two small, cash-neutral within-group tilts toward lower-vol, narrower-interval assets. (1) Shift 2pp from A10 to A02 within us_broad: A02 has 8.9% vol and 3.8% 5-day width vs A10's 14.6% and 6.0%, and a far better 20-day (-0.9% vs -5.0%). (2) Shift 2pp from A15 to A08 within financials: A08's 4.9% width and 13.3% vol dominate A15's 6.9% and 19.0%, and A15's recent tape (1d -1.8%, 20d -4.8%) is worse. All other weights are held at current levels and cash stays at 20.62%. Group exposures remain well under caps (us_broad 19.0%, financials 11.1%) and every increase above baseline is within the +15pp allowance.",
